<?xml version='1.0' encoding='UTF-8'?>
<chapter xml:id="functorial"><title>Homomorphisms and Lie subgroups</title>
<section xml:id="naturality"><title>Naturality of the exponential map</title>
<theorem xml:id="natural"><statement><p>If <m>f:G\to H</m> is a smooth group homomorphism and <m>df_e:\g\to\h</m> its differential at the identity, then</p><p><me>f(\exp_G(X))=\exp_H(df_eX).</me></p></statement><proof><p>Differentiating <m>f\circ L_g=L_{f(g)}\circ f</m> at <m>e</m> shows that <m>\widetilde X</m> is <m>f</m>-related to the left-invariant field determined by <m>df_eX</m>. By <xref ref="related-curves"/>, <m>t\mapsto f(\exp_G(tX))</m> is its integral curve through the identity. Uniqueness gives <m>f(\exp_G(tX))=\exp_H(tdf_eX)</m>; set <m>t=1</m>.</p></proof></theorem>
<theorem xml:id="uniqueness"><title>A differential determines a homomorphism</title><statement><p>If <m>G</m> is connected and smooth homomorphisms <m>f_1,f_2:G\to H</m> satisfy <m>d(f_1)_e=d(f_2)_e</m>, then <m>f_1=f_2</m>.</p></statement><proof><p>By <xref ref="natural"/>, the maps agree on <m>\exp_G(\g)</m>, which contains a neighborhood of <m>e</m> by <xref ref="local-diffeo"/>. Choose a symmetric identity neighborhood <m>U=U^{-1}</m> in this set. The subgroup <m>K=\bigcup_{n\geq1}U^n</m> is open; every coset is open, so its complement is open as well. Connectedness implies <m>K=G</m>. The homomorphisms agree on every finite product of elements of <m>U</m>, hence on <m>G</m>.</p></proof></theorem>
<example><title>Why connectedness matters</title><p>On the discrete Lie group <m>G=\{1,-1\}</m>, the identity homomorphism and the constant homomorphism to <m>1</m> have the same differential on the zero Lie algebra but are different maps.</p></example>
</section>
<section xml:id="subgroups"><title>Recognizing the Lie algebra of a subgroup</title>
<theorem xml:id="subgroup-test"><statement><p>Let <m>H\hookrightarrow G</m> be a Lie subgroup, and identify <m>\h</m> with its image in <m>\g</m>. For <m>X\in\g</m>, the following conditions are equivalent: <m>X\in\h</m>; <m>\exp_G(tX)\in H</m> for every real <m>t</m>; and <m>\exp_G(tX)\in H</m> for all <m>t</m> in some nonempty open interval.</p></statement><proof><p>If <m>X\in\h</m>, apply <xref ref="natural"/> to the inclusion to obtain <m>\exp_G(tX)=i(\exp_H(tX))</m> for every <m>t</m>. This implies the interval condition.</p><p>Conversely, take an interior point <m>t_0</m> of the given interval. For sufficiently small <m>s</m>, the group law shows that <m>\exp_G(sX)=\exp_G(-t_0X)\exp_G((t_0+s)X)\in H</m>. For an embedded subgroup, this is a smooth curve in <m>H</m>; differentiation at zero gives <m>X\in T_eH</m>. For an immersed Lie subgroup, use the initial-submanifold property of integral leaves for the distribution <m>\Delta_g=(dL_g)_e\h</m>: a smooth ambient curve whose image lies in the leaf <m>H</m> is smooth as a curve in that leaf. Differentiation again gives <m>X\in\h</m>.</p></proof></theorem>
<remark><p>For immersed subgroups, ambient smoothness alone is not a justification for smoothness into an arbitrary immersed manifold. The integral-leaf property is the additional input. All matrix subgroups used below are embedded, so the elementary embedded argument suffices there.</p></remark>
</section></chapter>

